In a SwiftUI view, call it with `.task { await model.load() }` so SwiftUI cancels the task when the view disappears.

With **Main Actor** default isolation, make sure `Parser.parse` and the helpers it calls are nonisolated. If the parser has no actor-bound state, you can opt the whole type out with `nonisolated enum Parser`. `Item` should conform to `Sendable` because the parsed values cross back to the main actor. The example assumes a throwing parser; if yours is nonthrowing, remove `throws` and the corresponding `try` expressions.

Use `URLSession` for asynchronous I/O and make `load()` async so the caller owns cancellation. Keep parsing on the main actor unless profiling shows it causes UI stalls.

```swift
import Foundation
import Observation

@Observable
final class FeedModel {
    private(set) var items: [Item] = []
    private(set) var isLoading = false
    private(set) var error: (any Error)?

    private let feedURL: URL

    init(feedURL: URL) {
        self.feedURL = feedURL
    }

    func load() async {
        guard !isLoading, !Task.isCancelled else { return }

        isLoading = true
        error = nil
        defer { isLoading = false }

        do {
            let (data, response) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: feedURL)
            try Task.checkCancellation()

            guard let http = response as? HTTPURLResponse,
                  (200..<300).contains(http.statusCode) else {
                throw URLError(.badServerResponse)
            }

            items = Parser.parse(data)
        } catch {
            // URLSession cancellation can surface as a URLError.
            guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }
            self.error = error
        }
    }
}
```

In SwiftUI, call it with `.task { await model.load() }` so SwiftUI cancels the load when the view disappears. The `isLoading` guard ignores overlapping calls while the request is suspended, and a failed refresh leaves the previous items intact. If `Parser.parse` throws, use `items = try Parser.parse(data)`.

With **Default Actor Isolation = MainActor**, the model needs no explicit `@MainActor` or dispatch back to the main queue. `URLSession` suspends the task during I/O; it doesn’t block the main actor. If parsing itself proves expensive, move just that work into an `@concurrent` helper, opt the parser out of main-actor isolation, and make `Item` a nonisolated `Sendable` value type so the results can cross back safely.

Use `URLSession` for async network I/O and let the caller own the task’s lifetime. With Swift 6.2’s Approachable Concurrency and Default Actor Isolation set to `MainActor`, the model can update its state directly—no dispatch queues or explicit `@MainActor` annotation needed.

